Treat the thin rod as an axial spring and the balls as the moving masses.

Rod cross-sectional area:

\[
A=\pi\left(\frac d2\right)^2
=\pi(5\times 10^{-4})^2
=7.85\times 10^{-7}\ \text{m}^2
\]

Axial spring constant of the rod:

\[
k=\frac{YA}{l}
=\frac{(2\times 10^{11})(7.85\times 10^{-7})}{0.10}
\approx 1.57\times 10^6\ \text{N/m}
\]

Mass of one steel ball:

\[
m=\rho \frac{4\pi r^3}{3}
=7800\cdot \frac{4\pi(0.01)^3}{3}
\approx 3.27\times 10^{-2}\ \text{kg}
\]

For the free longitudinal mode, the two equal balls move oppositely, so the relative coordinate has reduced mass \(m/2\). Thus

\[
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{k}{m/2}}=\sqrt{\frac{2k}{m}}
\]

and

\[
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{2k}}
\]

\[
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{3.27\times 10^{-2}}{2(1.57\times 10^6)}}
\approx 6.4\times 10^{-4}\ \text{s}
\]

\[
\boxed{T \approx 6.4\times 10^{-4}\ \text{s} \approx 0.64\ \text{ms}}
\]
